Task 1: Setting Up the Environment
Subtask 1.1: Install Required Dependencies
First, update your system and install essential tools:

# Update system packages
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install curl, wget, and other utilities
sudo apt install -y curl wget git vim unzip

# Install Docker
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh
sudo sh get-docker.sh
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker

# Verify Docker installation
docker --version
Subtask 1.2: Install kubectl
Install the Kubernetes command-line tool:

# Download kubectl
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"

# Make kubectl executable
chmod +x kubectl

# Move kubectl to PATH
sudo mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/

# Verify installation
kubectl version --client
Subtask 1.3: Install and Start Minikube
Set up a local Kubernetes cluster:

# Download Minikube
curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64

# Install Minikube
sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube

# Start Minikube cluster
minikube start --driver=docker --memory=4096 --cpus=2

# Verify cluster is running
kubectl cluster-info
kubectl get nodes
Subtask 1.4: Install Argo CD
Deploy Argo CD in your Kubernetes cluster:

# Create Argo CD namespace
kubectl create namespace argocd

# Install Argo CD
kubectl apply -n argocd -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml

# Wait for Argo CD pods to be ready
kubectl wait --for=condition=available --timeout=300s deployment/argocd-server -n argocd

# Verify Argo CD installation
kubectl get pods -n argocd
Subtask 1.5: Access Argo CD Web Interface
Configure access to the Argo CD web interface:

# Get the initial admin password
kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d && echo

# Port forward to access Argo CD UI (run in background)
kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443 &

# Install Argo CD CLI
curl -sSL -o argocd-linux-amd64 https://github.com/argoproj/argo-cd/releases/latest/download/argocd-linux-amd64
sudo install -m 555 argocd-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/argocd
rm argocd-linux-amd64

# Login to Argo CD CLI
argocd login localhost:8080 --username admin --password $(kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d) --insecure
Note: Save the admin password displayed above for accessing the web interface.

Task 2: Connecting Argo CD to GitHub
Subtask 2.1: Create a GitHub Repository
Create a sample repository for this lab:

# Create a local directory for your application
mkdir ~/argocd-demo-app
cd ~/argocd-demo-app

# Initialize Git repository
git init

# Create a simple application manifest
cat > deployment.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: demo-app
  labels:
    app: demo-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: demo-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: demo-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: demo-app
        image: nginx:1.21
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: demo-app-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: demo-app
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80
  type: ClusterIP
EOF

# Create application configuration
cat > application.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application
metadata:
  name: demo-app
  namespace: argocd
spec:
  project: default
  source:
    repoURL: https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/argocd-demo-app.git
    targetRevision: HEAD
    path: .
  destination:
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
    namespace: default
  syncPolicy:
    automated:
      prune: true
      selfHeal: true
EOF

# Add files to Git
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit: Add demo application manifests"
Manual Step:

Go to GitHub.com and create a new repository named argocd-demo-app
Make it public
Copy the repository URL
# Replace YOUR_USERNAME with your actual GitHub username
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/argocd-demo-app.git

# Update the application.yaml with your actual repository URL
sed -i 's/YOUR_USERNAME/your-actual-username/g' application.yaml

# Push to GitHub
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main
Subtask 2.2: Configure Repository Access in Argo CD
Add your GitHub repository to Argo CD:

# Add repository to Argo CD (replace with your repository URL)
argocd repo add https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/argocd-demo-app.git

# Verify repository is added
argocd repo list
For private repositories, you would need to add credentials:

# For private repositories (optional)
# argocd repo add https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/argocd-demo-app.git --username YOUR_USERNAME --password YOUR_TOKEN
Subtask 2.3: Verify Repository Connection
Check that Argo CD can access your repository:

# Test repository connection
argocd repo get https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/argocd-demo-app.git

# Check repository status
kubectl get secrets -n argocd | grep repo
Task 3: Deploy Sample Application from GitHub Repository
Subtask 3.1: Create Argo CD Application
Deploy your application using Argo CD:

# Create application from your GitHub repository
argocd app create demo-app \
  --repo https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/argocd-demo-app.git \
  --path . \
  --dest-server https://kubernetes.default.svc \
  --dest-namespace default \
  --sync-policy automated \
  --auto-prune \
  --self-heal

# Verify application creation
argocd app list
Subtask 3.2: Sync and Deploy Application
Synchronize your application with the repository:

# Sync the application
argocd app sync demo-app

# Check application status
argocd app get demo-app

# Watch the deployment
kubectl get pods -w
Subtask 3.3: Verify Deployment
Confirm that your application is running correctly:

# Check deployed resources
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get services
kubectl get pods

# Check application logs
kubectl logs -l app=demo-app

# Test the application
kubectl port-forward svc/demo-app-service 8081:80 &
curl http://localhost:8081
Subtask 3.4: Access Argo CD Web Interface
View your deployment in the Argo CD web interface:

Open a web browser
Navigate to https://localhost:8080
Login with username: admin and the password you saved earlier
Click on the demo-app application to view its status
Task 4: Testing GitOps Workflow
Subtask 4.1: Modify Application Configuration
Test the GitOps workflow by making changes:

# Navigate to your local repository
cd ~/argocd-demo-app

# Modify the deployment to use 3 replicas
sed -i 's/replicas: 2/replicas: 3/g' deployment.yaml

# Commit and push changes
git add deployment.yaml
git commit -m "Scale application to 3 replicas"
git push origin main
Subtask 4.2: Observe Automatic Synchronization
Watch Argo CD automatically sync the changes:

# Watch for changes in pods
kubectl get pods -l app=demo-app -w

# Check application status
argocd app get demo-app

# Verify 3 replicas are running
kubectl get deployment demo-app
Subtask 4.3: Test Application Rollback
Demonstrate rollback capabilities:

# View application history
argocd app history demo-app

# Rollback to previous version (if needed)
# argocd app rollback demo-app 1

# Check current status
argocd app get demo-app --show-params
Task 5: Monitoring and Management
Subtask 5.1: Monitor Application Health
Check application health and sync status:

# Get detailed application information
argocd app get demo-app

# Check sync status
argocd app wait demo-app --health

# View application events
kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
Subtask 5.2: Configure Notifications (Optional)
Set up basic monitoring:

# Check Argo CD server logs
kubectl logs -n argocd deployment/argocd-server

# Monitor application controller logs
kubectl logs -n argocd deployment/argocd-application-controller
Subtask 5.3: Clean Up Resources
Clean up the lab environment:

# Delete the application
argocd app delete demo-app --cascade

# Verify cleanup
kubectl get all

# Stop port forwarding processes
pkill -f "kubectl port-forward"
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Repository Connection Failed
Problem: Argo CD cannot connect to GitHub repository

Solution:

# Check repository URL is correct
argocd repo list

# Test manual git clone
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/argocd-demo-app.git /tmp/test-repo

# Check network connectivity
curl -I https://github.com
Issue 2: Application Sync Failed
Problem: Application fails to sync

Solution:

# Check application logs
argocd app logs demo-app

# Force refresh
argocd app get demo-app --refresh

# Manual sync
argocd app sync demo-app --force
Issue 3: Pods Not Starting
Problem: Application pods are not starting

Solution:

# Check pod status
kubectl describe pods -l app=demo-app

# Check resource constraints
kubectl top nodes
kubectl top pods

# Check events
kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=demo-app
Key Concepts Summary
GitOps: A deployment methodology that uses Git repositories as the single source of truth for infrastructure and application configuration.

Argo CD: A declarative, GitOps continuous delivery tool for Kubernetes that automatically syncs applications with their Git repository state.

Continuous Deployment: The practice of automatically deploying code changes to production after they pass automated tests.

Declarative Configuration: Describing the desired state of your system rather than the steps to achieve it.

